\chapter{Expressions and evaluation order}
MuLL evaluates operands and function arguments from left to right. Parentheses
override precedence. The precedence levels, from weakest to strongest, are:
\api{or}; \api{and}; \api{== !=}; \api{< > <= >=}; \api{+ -};
\api{* / \%}; unary \api{+ - ! not}; and member access, indexing, and calls.
Binary operators at the same level associate left to right.

\begin{mullcode}
G:Assert(2 + 3 * 4 == 14);
G:Assert((2 + 3) * 4 == 20);
G:Assert(10 - 3 - 2 == 5);
G:Assert(-5 % 2 == -1);
G:Assert(not false and !nil);
var order <- "";
val mark <- fn(s){ order <- order + s; return s; };
val pair <- [mark("a"), mark("b")];
G:Assert(order == "ab");
\end{mullcode}

Arithmetic and ordering require numbers. Addition also concatenates two
strings or two lists of the same kind. Equality accepts any pair of values
and follows the structural rules in Chapter 48. Remainder uses floating-point
remainder with the sign of the left operand.

\section{Short circuiting}
\api{a or b} returns \api{a} when it is true, otherwise evaluating and
returning \api{b}. \api{a and b} returns \api{a} when it is false, otherwise
evaluating and returning \api{b}. The unused expression produces neither
effects nor errors. Unary \api{!} and \api{not} return a Boolean negation.

Comparisons are ordinary binary expressions. Write \api{a < b and b < c}
for a range test; \api{a < b < c} would attempt to order a Boolean and a number.
